  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1233 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nSurface northerly winds shift back to the southeast bringing Gulf \nmoisture across the local area overnight into Saturday morning. And \nhere comes the next upper level system moving across the western \nFour Corners region and northwest Mexico. This upper level system \nslowly moves eastward over northern Mexico throughout the day and \nnight on Saturday. At the surface, increased moisture from the Gulf \npushes across the Rio Grande during the morning period with pwats in \nthe 1.6 to 1.7 inches and where rain chances start at 20 percent. \nMoving into the afternoon, increased moisture stays in place while \ninstability comes up, resulting in rain chances expanding eastward \nand ranging from 20 to 60 percent. Rain chances continue elevated \n(40 to 60) across the local area for Saturday evening with the \nwettest period forecast to be from Saturday evening into Sunday \nmorning. Rain chances diminish some for Sunday, but with the upper \nlevel system still to the west of the local area, wet conditions are \nlikely to persist into Monday. Temperatures over the weekend will be \ncool with highs in the 60s and low 70s and lows in the 50s. The Sun \nis going to have a hard time coming out with cloudy skies through \nthe period. Low confidence when it comes to Monday's forecast as \nmodels differ. For now, we are going to show rain chances between 20 \nand 30 percent for the I-35 corridor and Coastal Plains. All of this \nrain will be beneficial for the local area during a 48 to 72 hour \nperiod. Storm rainfall totals are forecast to range form a few \nhundredths over parts of the northern Hill Country to between one to \ntwo inches over the southern Edwards Plateau and Rio Grande. Austin \nand San Antonio metro areas could see from one quarter to an inch.\n\nDry weather looking to return on Tuesday with a warming trend mid \nweek but it is just slightly above values for this time of the year \n(highs: low 70s, lows: 50s).",
